    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Ask whether yesterday actually happened\ndeploy.sh checks the worker once, on the day you deploy. Nothing\nchecks it on day forty. A dead worker means no scheduled workflow\nand no mailbox sync, and the interface stays perfectly healthy the\nwhole time \u2014 so the only way to find it is to go and look.",
        "height": 400,
        "width": 540,
        "color": 5
      },
      "id": "648238124152",
      "name": "1. Ask whether yesterday actually happened",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -20,
        60
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Did the daily automations run?\nThree workflows are supposed to run every morning. Each is asked\nfor by name and time, not pulled off the latest page of every\nautomationRun in the CRM. Form intake writes a run on every\nsubmit; past twenty recent rows the schedules used to fall off\nthe page and this check reported a dead worker on a healthy one.",
        "height": 340,
        "width": 1080,
        "color": 6
      },
      "id": "490030893347",
      "name": "2. Did the daily automations run?",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        540,
        120
      ]
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Say something only when something is wrong\nA green tick every morning trains people to stop reading. This\nsends nothing on a good day, and names exactly what is missing on\na bad one. It does not check backup freshness \u2014 that is\nverify_restore.py, on a schedule the operator owns.",
        "height": 550,
        "width": 540,
        "color": 2
      },
      "id": "346905413336",
      "name": "3. Say something only when something is wrong",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        1640,
        100
      ]
    }
